<h2 class="p2-part">Part 1: Fun with Filters</h2>
<section id="part-1-1"><h3>Part 1.1: Convolutions from Scratch!</h3>
<p>For each output pixel, convolution multiplies a neighborhood of image pixels by a flipped filter and adds the products. I wrote two NumPy implementations. The first uses four loops: two over the image and two over the filter. The second keeps the image loops and uses NumPy to multiply and sum each neighborhood.</p>
<p>Both functions flip the filter horizontally and vertically. I add zero padding before convolution so the output has the same dimensions as the input. For an even filter dimension, the padding has one more pixel before the image than after it. This matches SciPy’s <code>mode="same"</code> alignment. Zero padding treats pixels outside the image as black, so the box blur darkens the border.</p>
<h4>Four loops</h4>
<pre><code>def convolve_four_loops(image, kernel):
    # Get image and filter dimensions
    height, width = image.shape
    kh, kw = kernel.shape

    # Flip filter vertically and horizontally
    flipped = kernel[::-1, ::-1]

    # Add zero padding so output keeps original image size
    padded = np.pad(image, ((kh // 2, (kh - 1) // 2), (kw // 2, (kw - 1) // 2)), mode=&quot;constant&quot;)

    # Create empty output image
    output = np.zeros((height, width))

    # Visit each output pixel
    for row in range(height):
        for col in range(width):
            # Multiply each nearby pixel by its filter weight
            for i in range(kh):
                for j in range(kw):
                    output[row, col] += padded[row + i, col + j] * flipped[i, j]

    return output</code></pre>
<h4>Two loops</h4>
<pre><code>def convolve_two_loops(image, kernel):
    # Get image and filter dimensions
    height, width = image.shape
    kh, kw = kernel.shape

    # Flip filter vertically and horizontally
    flipped = kernel[::-1, ::-1]

    # Add zero padding so output keeps original image size
    padded = np.pad(image, ((kh // 2, (kh - 1) // 2), (kw // 2, (kw - 1) // 2)), mode=&quot;constant&quot;)

    # Create empty output image
    output = np.zeros((height, width))

    # Visit each output pixel
    for row in range(height):
        for col in range(width):
            # Select image neighborhood covered by filter
            patch = padded[row:row + kh, col:col + kw]
            # Multiply corresponding values + add products
            output[row, col] = np.sum(patch * flipped)

    return output</code></pre>
<p>My 9 × 9 box filter gives all 81 pixels equal weight. Dividing by 81 keeps its weights summed to 1. I apply it to my 500 × 500 selfie and compare the outputs with <a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.signal.convolve2d.html">scipy.signal.convolve2d</a> using the same padding.</p>
<pre><code># Define 9x9 box filter
box_filter = np.ones((9, 9)) / 81</code></pre>
<pre><code># Apply box filter with four loops + measure runtime
start = perf_counter()
blurred_four = convolve_four_loops(selfie, box_filter)
four_time = perf_counter() - start

print(f&quot;Four-loop runtime: {four_time:.3f} seconds&quot;)

# Display blurred image
plt.imshow(blurred_four, cmap=&quot;gray&quot;, vmin=0, vmax=1)
plt.title(&quot;9x9 box blur: four loops&quot;)</code></pre>
<pre><code># Apply box filter with two loops + measure runtime
start = perf_counter()
blurred_two = convolve_two_loops(selfie, box_filter)
two_time = perf_counter() - start

print(f&quot;Two-loop runtime: {two_time:.3f} seconds&quot;)

# Display blurred image
plt.imshow(blurred_two, cmap=&quot;gray&quot;, vmin=0, vmax=1)
plt.title(&quot;9x9 box blur: two loops&quot;)</code></pre>
<pre><code># Apply box filter with SciPy + measure runtime
