<section id="part-1-3"><h3>Part 1.3: Derivative of Gaussian (DoG) Filter</h3>
<p>Finite differences respond to small changes in brightness, including texture in the grass. I first smooth the image with a Gaussian filter. I use <code>cv2.getGaussianKernel(19, 3)</code> and multiply the column by its transpose to make a 19 × 19 filter with σ = 3. Pixels near the center receive more weight than pixels near the edge.</p>
<div class="p2-grid p2-cols-2">
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-2/gaussian-kernel.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-2/gaussian-kernel.png" width="19" height="19" loading="lazy" alt="Gaussian filter: 19 × 19, σ = 3"></a><figcaption>Gaussian filter: 19 × 19, σ = 3</figcaption></figure>
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-2/cameraman-blurred.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-2/cameraman-blurred.png" width="540" height="542" loading="lazy" alt="Gaussian-smoothed cameraman"></a><figcaption>Gaussian-smoothed cameraman</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p>I take finite differences of the smoothed image and calculate the gradient magnitude again. Smoothing reduces fine texture and spreads the remaining edges over more pixels. It also lowers their peak strength, so I use a smaller threshold of 0.03. A threshold of 0.04 removed more of the outline than I wanted.</p>
<div class="p2-grid p2-cols-1">
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-2/blurred-magnitude.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-2/blurred-magnitude.png" width="540" height="542" loading="lazy" alt="Smoothed gradient magnitude"></a><figcaption>Smoothed gradient magnitude</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<div class="p2-grid p2-cols-1">
<figure><a href="/images/cs180/project-2/cameraman-smoothed-edges.png"><img src="/images/cs180/project-2/cameraman-smoothed-edges.png" width="540" height="542" loading="lazy" alt="Smoothed binary edges: threshold 0.03"></a><figcaption>Smoothed binary edges: threshold 0.03</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<p>I then convolve the Gaussian filter with D<sub>x</sub> and D<sub>y</sub> using <code>mode="full"</code>. These derivative of Gaussian (DoG) filters combine smoothing and differentiation. Convolution is associative: (I ∗ G) ∗ D = I ∗ (G ∗ D). I can apply each DoG filter directly to the original cameraman image.</p>
