<h2 class="p2-part">Part 2: Fun with Frequencies!</h2>
<section id="part-2-1"><h3>Part 2.1: Image "Sharpening"</h3>
<p>A Gaussian blur reduces fine detail. I subtract the blurred image from the original to isolate high frequencies, then add a scaled copy of those details back to the original. The sharpened image is I + α(I − G ∗ I). Here, I is the original image, G is the Gaussian blur filter, and ∗ means convolution. G ∗ I is the blurred image, so I − G ∗ I contains the high-frequency details. The sharpening amount α controls how much of that detail I add back. With α = 0, the image stays unchanged; larger positive values increase the sharpening.</p>
<p>I combine these operations into one convolution with the filter (1 + α)δ − αG. The impulse filter δ has a 1 at its center and zeros elsewhere, so convolving it with I returns I unchanged. This combined filter produces (1 + α)I − α(G ∗ I), which is the same sharpening formula written above.</p>
<p>I use a 7 × 7 Gaussian with σ = 1 and symmetric boundary extension. After sharpening, I clip values to [0, 1]. The high-frequency displays use gray for zero so both positive and negative details remain visible.</p>
