<section id="part-2-2"><h3>Part 2.2: Hybrid Images</h3>
<p>A hybrid image combines the low frequencies of one image with the high frequencies of another. I blur the first image to keep its low frequencies. I subtract a blurred copy of the second image from itself to keep its high frequencies, then add these two components. The first image’s broad shapes are easier to see from farther away, while the second image’s fine details are easier to see up close. This implements the Gaussian filtering method in Section 2 of <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20070315210101/http://cvcl.mit.edu/hybrid/OlivaTorralb_Hybrid_Siggraph06.pdf">Oliva, Torralba, and Schyns’s Hybrid Images</a>.</p>
<p>I use the starter <code>align_images</code> function and select corresponding eyes to align the faces. The function adjusts position, scale, and rotation, then crops the images to a shared size. This follows the paper’s discussion in Section 2.2: matching features and pose helps the two images form a coherent face.</p>
<p>Each Gaussian extends three standard deviations from its center. I filter vertically and horizontally with symmetric boundary extension. The σ values below are in pixels after alignment. A larger σ makes a wider blur and a lower low-pass cutoff. For the high-pass component, subtracting that wider blur retains a broader range of detail.</p>
