<section id="part-2-4"><h3>Part 2.4: Multiresolution Blending (a.k.a. the Oraple!)</h3>
<p>I use the mask-based blend in Section 3.2 of Burt and Adelson, on page 230. I build Gaussian stacks for the mask and both input images, then obtain Laplacian stacks for the images. At each level, B<sub>i</sub> = M<sub>i</sub>L<sub>A,i</sub> + (1 − M<sub>i</sub>)L<sub>B,i</sub>.</p>
<p>Here, i is the stack level, and A and B label the two input images. L<sub>A,i</sub> and L<sub>B,i</sub> are their Laplacian stack values at that level. M<sub>i</sub> is the corresponding Gaussian mask level, and B<sub>i</sub> is the blended result at that level. I multiply corresponding pixels: M<sub>i</sub> weights image A’s contribution, and 1 − M<sub>i</sub> weights image B’s contribution. A mask value of 1 selects A, a value of 0 selects B, and values between them mix both. The final image is the sum of B<sub>i</sub> over all six levels.</p>
<p>The mask is sharper at fine levels and more blurred at coarse levels. This keeps small details near the selected boundary while mixing broad brightness changes over a wider region. Since the stacks have matching dimensions, I combine corresponding pixels directly and sum the levels.</p>
